<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net9.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <AssemblyName>wiki</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Wiki</RootNamespace>
    <StartupObject>Wiki.App</StartupObject>
    <!-- Three targets, because a target framework is the FLOOR of runtime
         support and roll-forward only ever moves UP: a net10.0 assembly
         cannot load on a 9.x runtime, no matter how it is configured. So
         "ship the latest" and "don't strand anyone on 8.x/9.x" cannot both be
         served by a single target - hence all three.

         Where it actually matters is narrower than it looks. The release
         binaries and the Docker image carry self-contained native AOT and
         need no runtime installed at all, so their consumers are indifferent
         to this list; CI publishes those from the newest target (-f, see
         .github/workflows/ci.yml). The one channel where a consumer's
         installed runtime decides anything is the `dotnet tool` package
         below, which ships IL - and a multi-targeted tool nupkg carries
         tools/net8.0, tools/net9.0 and tools/net10.0, so `dotnet tool
         install` resolves whichever matches the machine.

         The code costs nothing for this: it compiles warning-free (AOT
         analyzers included) against all three, because the dependency
         surface is System.CommandLine + Spectre.Console and a handful of
         stable BCL primitives. Adding an API newer than the lowest target is
         what would break the arrangement - that is the constraint this list
         imposes, and the reason to drop net8.0 if it ever becomes worth it.

         RollForward is still needed on top: without it each build demands its
         EXACT runtime and refuses to start on a machine that has, say, 8.0
         and 10.0 but not 9.0 ("You must install or update .NET to run this
         application") even though the assemblies are fine. It sets the
         ceiling; TargetFrameworks sets the floor. -->
    <RollForward>LatestMajor</RollForward>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- `dotnet pack` produces a global tool for the GitHub Packages NuGet feed.
       That channel ships the IL build, not the AOT one - `dotnet tool` has no
       native-AOT story - so it needs a .NET runtime present: 8.0, 9.0 or
       10.0, whichever the machine has, resolved from the matching tools/<tfm>
       folder in the nupkg. The release
       binaries stay the recommended install; this exists for `dotnet tool`
       users and CI images that already have the SDK. PackAsTool is inert
       during `dotnet publish -r <rid>`, so the AOT build is unaffected. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>wiki</ToolCommandName>
    <PackageId>LlmWiki.Cli</PackageId>
    <!-- PackageVersion, not Version: the nupkg gets its own rolling version
         (CI passes a -p:PackageVersion of 1.0.0-main.RUNNUMBER) while the
         assembly version, and so the version the release binaries report,
         stays exactly what it was. -->
    <PackageVersion>1.0.0</PackageVersion>
    <Authors>Joran Bergfeld</Authors>
    <Description>A local, CLI-operated LLM wiki: the `wiki` CLI that builds and maintains a vault of interlinked markdown from immutable raw sources.</Description>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/JoranBergfeld/llm-wiki</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/JoranBergfeld/llm-wiki.git</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageTags>wiki;llm;cli;knowledge-base;markdown</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.CommandLine" Version="2.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Spectre.Console" Version="0.49.1" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Templates/*.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="../../README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />
    <None Include="../../LICENSE.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
